#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124411 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/id #uuid "f7b85641-289b-57b2-9ffc-ff4c5e10025a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8572" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n![](images/af/af05cedf7b2015f4f61df71e833390aa845d50126c3203369320ac6feb8b2cc3.svg)\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124412 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124600 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "854f39a0-99b3-59f3-a9d7-4909850254ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-8573" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n![](images/6e/6e51c22c33c32d52c51657552a3c174d706a316ec10c78a4a953d2fcebc304ff.svg)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix ![](images/0e/0e25fe1b46b828e6fac168d1755af7aed9a7c3f657d1a6d6677fd71746b7ce31.svg) we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1= -{2}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](images/54/54dad1c9d747fead6f27dd0e9af7cca434c40a83d56c02c854bb610a1cdc6087.svg)\n\nBy setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/d2/d28cf572126716928bc9ded7c2646871540e20b1d93d1efc929599b4c379d38b.svg)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124413 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124601 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "cb76e9dc-1195-58d3-8e58-a3421770265a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8574" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n![](images/3d/3da867d461c2b42c21badd67ce59c9ec5f6448597b4d2dcbb7e8b28ffe397768.svg) \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{8}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/04/04f6efaa2bb141940040a04905e52aacd7aa739e1bd58dd71666a10543ff0f74.svg)\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector ![](images/dd/dd78cbf2025fdf012dc27429596c84be09825b436c32a5410f50f78cd4943a66.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n![](images/5b/5b5b06d4cd243ca0370807b58bc9d0da68f34aa008956016bab3da29377df219.svg)\nSetting  $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector  ![](images/3e/3ea698aa474cb6d28b3fb511215fbe5da7d01b6653e884719d99483eac5635a0.svg)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n![](images/b7/b736228d68ad58b430f707236014be76e0354bf42ff9fcd73c495a077835bad7.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124414 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124602 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "7959b6aa-7ef0-5ba1-b74b-5e59481d0145" :question/math-academy-id "e-8575" :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](images/4e/4e737b78109f94302bdc256b4a8bb9585fdb214ccc89bd9439b52fa4973f59ba.svg) You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A,$ and\n![](images/6b/6b4045bf6fc71339953a55a1e3660384a569861537ee37427be110aa3442a872.svg) ![](images/34/34760505c150775e9b579151b333cf3baa044102e0197b4e9aae3444c0581cd0.svg) are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$ and $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3},$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](images/42/427a788b1c9f78e8ce25f06b54cef549d7be04757f6275cc66f12bd48652e40b.svg)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](images/bd/bdf233e22e7972a4eb912463bc81727d7e22b160ff823ec51d0be494d24d493c.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041d9841-9f08-519f-a78e-df5ce0f663c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269307} {:answer/id #uuid "1f0e42ec-d22c-5f4b-98ae-1c49098bd803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269529} {:answer/id #uuid "8c630ebe-0288-5d13-9598-c81879223762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270486} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8ff8df-8e33-5284-853b-8a9d4bc96442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270813} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ef856f-43ae-59d1-88a2-562a7bf18b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4ef856f-43ae-59d1-88a2-562a7bf18b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271287} :answer-field/id #uuid "87395b4b-a632-5a49-a2c8-1ed2a57ffd8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fb633b8-d688-5aba-9f36-3226f86d1384" :question/math-academy-id "q-140040" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-9\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -32+36 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-2I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124600 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e74244-76d5-5343-aca6-e7bd59feee49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321581} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf11105-9dd4-5e81-99c6-0f8f67e497ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321755} {:answer/id #uuid "75160b3c-6e20-57fe-b349-766ce20c700e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322060} {:answer/id #uuid "dab06ec1-2cd0-5fdf-b518-39c2bef4dd3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322858} {:answer/id #uuid "e1f9a34b-5bad-564b-910d-f9c95d4e1493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cf11105-9dd4-5e81-99c6-0f8f67e497ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321755} :answer-field/id #uuid "de0c8b55-2245-569b-a6f7-434321067ed8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4c8798a-e8e3-519c-a948-2cb26050a7a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37108" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$   find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$  \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323923 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124601 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a40dfb-f5fb-5a03-91a7-67d5b50e2c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321586} {:answer/id #uuid "42a07f5d-91e9-5805-a0c2-cc05cc5065db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321675} {:answer/id #uuid "6627dac0-8aa6-5c00-aa71-b20a0ef62004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321938} {:answer/id #uuid "c840ee09-15a6-500d-a880-9fb9b42776c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322700} {:answer/id #uuid "df744e3a-e5ca-59e6-a410-6bbb5cdddc93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6627dac0-8aa6-5c00-aa71-b20a0ef62004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321938} :answer-field/id #uuid "3378505f-63b2-5e06-9d7e-9f7ac4c9618b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67c100f5-0b85-5947-a038-3b4c63f6e781" :question/math-academy-id "q-37608" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal:  \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124602 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0acfb64f-d399-5128-abd9-0fd718785c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321233} {:answer/id #uuid "32ca874e-c218-5348-8254-ebdd24f6b5a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321535} {:answer/id #uuid "3d45e436-1b6c-5c26-990c-2a9bafba337d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=15,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321624} {:answer/id #uuid "824319fd-3efa-515c-be89-065ca8419d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322173} {:answer/id #uuid "f428ad45-b7f1-5420-a618-a7f180e0107e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "824319fd-3efa-515c-be89-065ca8419d86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322173} :answer-field/id #uuid "c580f2eb-b112-550e-99c4-120b3d89a440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52d32399-93d9-5ce5-a996-5d60c1e0900e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37610" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=15$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=15,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=15.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf3be306-cf4b-505f-9749-8c4d421cf8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379812} {:answer/id #uuid "675738a1-186a-5034-b012-faa1a6463030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379813} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d147f9-5e98-5cd7-a62a-3b200ed85486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379814} {:answer/id #uuid "40744a34-ee79-5f47-96a8-dad2d3f736ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379815} {:answer/id #uuid "b7371e87-7e47-503f-b703-0bb0dbb9110d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7371e87-7e47-503f-b703-0bb0dbb9110d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379816} :answer-field/id #uuid "95cac241-a6a2-5133-bd5b-0bc96d341951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3ab5a48-4ec0-5b84-8cff-a429fb97fc59" :question/math-academy-id "q-37631" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-(3)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-1I)\\text{x}=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124602 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "218d2232-b6b4-5353-9b44-bc2b615b173f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345651} {:answer/id #uuid "54242d0d-9f70-50fb-a1c7-1d7500836483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346065} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d2abcd-e82b-5bca-8c24-21c2c07d708d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346799} {:answer/id #uuid "a6178168-bb39-5a85-9081-d612835e2505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346802} {:answer/id #uuid "ff40704f-1d0b-5ea5-8bf6-a3b2c43d0da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "218d2232-b6b4-5353-9b44-bc2b615b173f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345651} :answer-field/id #uuid "e355db22-060d-55f4-890a-5652cc9577f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "def1c542-80f5-5b03-88dc-ad7a755f7e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80371" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $K$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}b & 1 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}b & 1 \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nK=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get  $a=-5,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124600 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b30d639-ee9f-5dd6-a783-78b787a8dd54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399126} {:answer/id #uuid "f91f8571-a85e-5261-a33d-4e0eb8050300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399127} {:answer/id #uuid "931df98b-abe6-5c33-9026-a64e621c7860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399128} {:answer/id #uuid "b46fbb27-12de-55de-8760-7556c63e5e1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399129} {:answer/id #uuid "3a9bca69-8e29-54e8-841b-6cdbc3697e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f91f8571-a85e-5261-a33d-4e0eb8050300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399127} :answer-field/id #uuid "a57196c6-f2e4-585a-92ce-6d08ae5e8dd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "912d4ab2-e55a-5e9a-8ddf-b1e59d14eb18" :question/math-academy-id "q-80467" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$   find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$  \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348394 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124601 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a5c262-cc22-5983-a61f-511098951290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345372} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa8ba48-0f15-5b2c-84b8-a833da307fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346746} {:answer/id #uuid "a9910791-e486-5fd3-bb75-79cd1d4e8819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346831} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3d5ea0-19f6-5122-82a9-c2add49ba24b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$E$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346871} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb53f77-ac95-5079-afcd-085b46b877ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9910791-e486-5fd3-bb75-79cd1d4e8819" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346831} :answer-field/id #uuid "530dc008-9954-52d4-b786-4fab0747c350" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "050f4d1f-7ad3-50e3-a5b8-1b02cf764a27" :question/math-academy-id "q-80480" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=E,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $E=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $E$ using its characteristic equation $det(E-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )-9\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $E.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -8\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
